(>> Solution to Question 1:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Explain Server-Side Web Architecture, contrasting server-side scripting \(PHP\) with client-side scripting \(JavaScript\) and detailing the HTTP lifecycle.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Web applications operate on a distributed client-server architecture:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   1. Server-Side Scripting \(PHP, Python, Node.js\):) Tj
T*
(   - Execution Environment: Executes entirely on the web server \(Apache/Nginx\) before any data is) Tj
T*
(   sent over the network.) Tj
T*
(   - Capabilities: Has direct access to server operating system resources, filesystem, protected) Tj
T*
(   databases \(MySQL\), external web services, and cryptographic secrets.) Tj
T*
(   - Output: Dynamically generates standard HTML, CSS, or JSON, which is transmitted across HTTP) Tj
T*
(   to the client. The client never sees the underlying PHP source code.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Client-Side Scripting \(JavaScript\):) Tj
T*
(   - Execution Environment: Executes inside the user's browser sandbox on the local device.) Tj
T*
(   - Capabilities: Manipulates the Document Object Model \(DOM\), captures user events, executes) Tj
T*
(   animations, and dispatches asynchronous AJAX/Fetch requests.) Tj
T*
(   - Security Sandbox: Lacks direct filesystem or database access; code is completely visible to) Tj
T*
(   anyone inspecting DevTools.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   The HTTP Lifecycle:) Tj
T*
(   Client browser dispatches HTTP GET/POST -> Web server routes request to PHP-FPM / FastCGI ->) Tj
T*
(   PHP executes code, queries MySQL database, and constructs HTML/JSON -> Web server returns HTTP) Tj
T*
(   200 OK response -> Browser parses HTML, builds DOM/CSSOM, and renders page.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 2:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Explain PHP Superglobal Arrays, detailing the purpose and security handling of $_GET, $_POST, $_SERVER, $_SESSION, $_COOKIE, and $_FILES.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Superglobals are built-in array variables that are globally accessible across all scopes) Tj
T*
(   without requiring `global $var;` declarations:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   1. $_GET: Associative array of variables passed via URL query parameters) Tj
T*
(   \(`/index.php?category=books&page=2`\). Vulnerable to manipulation; must never carry credentials.) Tj
T*
(   2. $_POST: Associative array of data submitted via HTTP POST request body. Used for) Tj
T*
(   state-modifying actions, form submissions, and login credentials.) Tj
T*
(   3. $_SERVER: Contains server environment metadata \(e.g., `$_SERVER['REQUEST_METHOD']`,) Tj
T*
(   `$_SERVER['REMOTE_ADDR']`, `$_SERVER['HTTP_HOST']`\).) Tj
T*
(   4. $_SESSION: Stores user session state across multiple page requests on the server.) Tj
T*
(   5. $_COOKIE: Contains cookies sent by client browser in the HTTP request header.) Tj
T*
(   6. $_FILES: Multi-dimensional array detailing uploaded files \(`name`, `type`, `size`,) Tj
T*
(   `tmp_name`, `error`\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   Security Mandate: All superglobal inputs represent untrusted user data. They must undergo) Tj
T*
(   strict Input Sanitization \(`filter_var\(\)`\), Parameterized Binding \(PDO\), and Context-Aware) Tj
T*
(   Output Encoding \(`htmlspecialchars\(\)`\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 3:) Tj
T*
ET
