(   [Problem Statement]: Describe Database Indexing principles \(B-Tree, Hash, Composite Indexes\) and explain how EXPLAIN queries identify table scan bottlenecks.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Database performance in server-side web engineering hinges upon index optimization:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   1. B-Tree Indexes \(Default in MySQL InnoDB\):) Tj
T*
(   - Balanced multi-way search tree where data is sorted and balanced at uniform depth.) Tj
T*
(   - Supports fast equality searches \(`=`\) and range searches \(`BETWEEN`, `<`, `>`, `LIKE) Tj
T*
(   'prefix%'`\) in O\(log N\) time.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Composite Indexes \(Multi-Column\):) Tj
T*
(   - Indexes spanning multiple columns: `INDEX idx_user_status \(user_id, status\)`.) Tj
T*
(   - Leftmost Prefix Rule: The index accelerates queries filtering on `\(user_id\)` or `\(user_id,) Tj
T*
(   status\)`, but CANNOT accelerate queries filtering on `\(status\)` alone!) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. Profiling with EXPLAIN:) Tj
T*
(   Prepending `EXPLAIN` to a SQL query outputs execution plan diagnostics:) Tj
T*
(   - `type = ALL`: Full Table Scan \(slowest; reads every disk row\).) Tj
T*
(   - `type = ref` / `eq_ref` / `range`: Index scan \(fast\).) Tj
T*
(   - `key`: Identifies which index was selected by query optimizer.) Tj
T*
(   - `rows`: Estimated number of rows MySQL must examine to produce the result.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
