(   [Problem Statement]: Explain Database Connection Pooling and persistent connections in PHP, detailing why FastCGI and PHP-FPM architectures require connection sizing.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   PHP operates on a Share-Nothing architecture where scripts execute, terminate, and tear down) Tj
T*
(   memory at the end of each HTTP request:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   The Persistent Connection Architecture \(`PDO::ATTR_PERSISTENT`\):) Tj
T*
(   - Instead of destroying the TCP connection to MySQL upon script exit, the PHP worker process) Tj
T*
(   keeps the connection open in an internal pool cache.) Tj
T*
(   - Subsequent requests served by the same PHP-FPM child process reuse the existing open) Tj
T*
(   connection immediately, saving 5-15ms of TCP/SSL handshake latency.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   The Concurrency Danger \(Max Connections Exhaustion\):) Tj
T*
(   - If PHP-FPM runs 200 child processes and each holds a persistent connection, MySQL requires at) Tj
T*
(   least 200 connection slots.) Tj
T*
(   - If multiple web servers exist, 5 web servers * 200 workers = 1,000 persistent connections,) Tj
T*
(   which can easily crash MySQL's `max_connections` limit.) Tj
T*
(   - Solution: Use dedicated middleware connection poolers \(e.g., ProxySQL or AWS RDS Proxy\)) Tj
T*
(   rather than raw persistent PDO connections in high-scale environments.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
